# CSS Selector Combinations

## 1. Grouping selectors

Use commas to apply the same styles to multiple selectors.

```css
h1, p, h2 {
  color: blue;
}
```

## 2. Child combinator (`>`)

Selects only direct children of a parent.

```css
.box > p {
  color: black;
}
```

## 3. Descendant combinator (space)

Selects matching elements anywhere inside another element, including nested descendants.

```css
.box .title {
  color: black;
}
```

## 4. Chaining selectors

Combines selectors without a space to target the same element.

```css
li.done {
  color: green;
}
```

Targets an `li` element with the class `done`.

## 5. Combining selector patterns

Selectors can be combined to narrow down the target.

```css
ul > li.done {
  color: green;
}
```

Targets `li` elements with class `done` that are direct children of a `ul`.

## Key takeaways

- `h1, h2` — grouping
- `.box > p` — direct child
- `.box p` — descendant
- `li.done` — same element, multiple conditions
- `ul > li.done` — combined selectors and relationships

Today's practice focused on selecting the intended elements precisely and avoiding unintended styling.
